Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
>    I don't. I agree with Kent --- there's a reason the specialized
> key types were removed from the game, and it's because they eat up
> inventory slots and add no challenge to the game. (Why not simply make
> the skeleton keys three or four times rarer, and three or four times
> heavier? It would do the same thing.)

New key types that aren't known initially and must be identified make
it harder to take keys for granted.

Making keys heavy breaks realism.  NHINRL, but it's not grossly
incorrect either.

The new keys would be important in cases where the player didn't want
to use the alternate means to get into safes or past metal doors, or
they weren't available.

Further, the suggestion is not the same as the old use for keys, in
which individual doors had locks that took different keys but were
otherwise identical.  All wooden doors and normal chests open with
skeleton keys.


>    However, I'm neutral on the safes. They seem not out of character,
> and they might provide some interesting interactions.
>
>    Wands of opening should never work on safes; and "knock" only
> when cast at very high skill levels. "The safe door jiggles."

The knock thing perhaps, but wands of opening are not actually that
common, and could stand being a bit more useful.

> Skeleton keys should work only rarely on safes; after all, they're
> designed to prevent thieves from breaking in, so obviously they'd
> resist skeleton keys!

According to the idea, skeleton keys would never work on safes, a
different (probably rarer) kind of key would be used for them

